# How to Use This Document

## What this topic covers

Topic 3 is syllabus **Head IV — Feminist Movements**. It has three strands:

1. the **waves of feminism in the West** — first, second, third and (since about 2012) fourth;
2. the **United Nations and women** — the world conferences, CEDAW and the global agenda;
3. the **women's movement in Pakistan** — from the Pakistan Movement to the Aurat March.

Topic 2 taught the *theories* of feminism. This topic teaches the *movements* — the people, organisations, campaigns, protests and laws through which those ideas changed the world.

In the descriptive paper Topic 3 has had ==9.5 visits== in eleven papers: nine full questions and one short note. Two families carry it. The **waves** have been asked five times (with one exact repeat, 2019 and 2023). The **movement in Pakistan** has been asked four times, plus a note on APWA and WAF and a question on **NGO-isation** (2026). It also supplies **22 of the 140 MCQs** — mostly dates: APWA ==1949==, Hudood ==1979==, CEDAW ==1996==, Beijing ==1995==.

## How this document is built

1. **Part Zero** gives you the words of the topic, the wave metaphor and its critics, and the whole topic on one timeline.
2. **Part One** has eleven chapters: four on the waves, one comparing them, one on the United Nations, three on Pakistan (roots, phases, the influence of the West), one on NGO-isation and one evaluating the movement.
3. **Part Two** sets out today's debates.
4. **Part Three** lists every past question, what the record shows, and five likely questions.
5. **Part Four** is a bank of about 120 numbered one-liners in the style of the past MCQs, followed by every past MCQ on the topic.
6. **Part Five** is the revision sheet — the whole topic in a few pages, every word explained.

::: simple
A **movement** is many people working together, over years, to change society. Feminism as a movement has come in bursts of energy — the "waves" — each with its own demands. Learn each wave by **what women lacked, what they demanded, how they campaigned and what they won**.
:::

# Part Zero — The ABC of Topic 3

## 0.1  The words of this topic

::: table | The key terms of Topic 3 (★ = you must be able to define it in one sentence).
@widths 3.4,10.6
Term | Simple meaning, with an example
Women's movement ★ | Collective, organised action by women (and men) over time to improve women's rights and status.
Wave ★ | A period of intense feminist activity with shared demands. *The first wave fought for the vote.*
Suffrage ★ | The **right to vote** in public elections. *New Zealand gave women suffrage in 1893.*
Suffragist | A campaigner for votes for women using **peaceful, legal** methods (petitions, meetings).
Suffragette | A British campaigner of the **WSPU** who used **militant** methods (window-smashing, hunger strikes).
Legal personhood | Being recognised by law as a person who can own property, sign contracts and go to court.
Coverture | The old English rule that a married woman's legal identity was **covered by her husband's**.
Reproductive rights ★ | The right to decide whether and when to have children, with access to contraception and safe care.
Consciousness-raising | Women meeting in small groups to discover that private problems are political.
Backlash | A reaction against feminist gains (Susan Faludi, *Backlash*, ==1991==).
Postfeminism | The claim that equality has been won and feminism is no longer needed.
Hashtag activism ★ | Campaigning through social media tags. *#MeToo (2017).*
CSW | The UN **Commission on the Status of Women**, founded in ==1946==.
CEDAW ★ | The **Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women** (==1979==) — "an international bill of rights for women" (MCQ 2026). Pakistan acceded in ==1996==.
Accession / ratification | Two ways a state becomes bound by a treaty. **Accession** = joining a treaty already in force without having signed it first.
Reservation | A statement by which a state excludes or limits part of a treaty for itself.
Beijing Platform for Action ★ | The plan adopted at the Fourth World Conference on Women (==1995==), with **12 critical areas of concern**.
Gender mainstreaming ★ | Putting a gender perspective into **all** policies and programmes, not only "women's projects".
APWA ★ | **All Pakistan Women's Association**, founded in ==1949== by **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** — welfare and reform.
WAF ★ | **Women's Action Forum**, founded in ==1981== to resist **Zia-ul-Haq's** discriminatory laws.
Hudood Ordinances ★ | Laws of ==1979== on zina, theft, drinking and false accusation; criticised for punishing rape victims.
Aurat March ★ | Annual women's march held on ==8 March== in Pakistani cities since ==2018==.
NGO-isation ★ | The shift of a social movement into **professional, donor-funded NGOs**, often said to depoliticise it.
Bodily autonomy | A person's right to decide about their own body. *The Aurat March slogan "Mera Jism Meri Marzi".*
State feminism | Women's rights advanced **through the state** — ministries, commissions, laws — rather than street protest.
:::

## 0.2  The wave metaphor — and its critics

The American journalist **Martha Weinman Lear** wrote of "**The Second Feminist Wave**" in the *New York Times Magazine* in ==1968==. The earlier campaign for the vote then became the "first wave". Later activists spoke of a third (==1992==) and a fourth (c. ==2012==).

::: fig flow | The four waves in one line.
@per 4
First wave (c. 1848–1920): votes, property, education
Second wave (1960s–1980s): work, family, body, sexuality
Third wave (1990s–2000s): diversity, identity, culture
Fourth wave (c. 2012–): digital campaigns against harassment
:::

::: balance | Is the wave metaphor useful?
- **For:** it gives a simple map of changing demands, and each wave did have its own leaders, methods and gains.
- **Against:** it follows **white, Western, middle-class** history; it hides activism **between** the waves (the "doldrums" of the 1920s–50s were full of campaigns for peace, labour and birth control); it makes each generation seem to reject the one before; and it ignores feminisms in Asia, Africa and Latin America, whose timing was different.
-> Use the waves as a **map, not a law**. In Pakistan, the "waves" arrived in a different order and mixed together (Chapter 9).
:::

## 0.3  The whole topic on one timeline

::: fig timeline | Topic 3 on one page: the West, the United Nations and Pakistan.
@layout vertical
== The West
1848: Seneca Falls Convention (first wave begins)
1893: New Zealand — women's vote
1920: USA — 19th Amendment; 1928: equal votes in Britain
1963: Friedan, The Feminine Mystique (second wave)
1973: Roe v. Wade
1992: Rebecca Walker, "Becoming the Third Wave"
2017: #MeToo goes global (fourth wave)
== The United Nations
1946: Commission on the Status of Women
1975: International Women's Year; Mexico City conference
1979: CEDAW adopted (in force 1981)
1995: Beijing — Platform for Action
2010: UN Women created
== Pakistan
1949: APWA founded
1961: Muslim Family Laws Ordinance
1979: Hudood Ordinances; 1981: WAF founded
1983: 12 February protest in Lahore
1996: Pakistan accedes to CEDAW
2006: Protection of Women Act
2018: First Aurat March
:::

::: eye | How the examiner uses this topic
Four of the five questions on the waves also ask how they **influenced Pakistan** or **affected women's lives**. Every question on Pakistan comes with a twist: **strengths and weaknesses** (2016), **past versus emerging trends** (2024), the shift from **legal rights to bodily autonomy** (2026), and **NGO-isation** (2026). Western history alone is half an answer. Always carry the story to Pakistan, and always evaluate.
:::
